#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7030", unit: "M2")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U2 Functors",[Prove every functor preserves isomorphisms.],[Apply it to both inverse equations.],[The image of an inverse is an inverse.],[If $g f=1$ and $f g=1$, then functoriality gives $F g F f=F(g f)=1$ and $F f F g=F(f g)=1$. Thus $F f$ is an isomorphism with inverse $F g$.],[Use identity preservation as well as composition preservation.]),
 p(2,"E","U7 Unit and counit",[For free vector spaces, describe the unit at a set $X$ and counit at a vector space $V$.],[Distinguish generators from formal sums of actual vectors.],[Insertion and evaluation are the two maps.],[The unit sends $x$ to its basis vector $[x]$ in the underlying set of $F X$. The counit sends $sum_i a_i[v_i]$ in $F U V$ to the actual vector sum $sum_i a_i v_i$ in $V$.],[Give the source and target of each map.]),
 p(3,"C","U5 Coequalizers",[Compute the coequalizer in vector spaces of $f,g:K -> K^2$ given by $f(t)=(t,0)$ and $g(t)=(0,t)$.],[Quotient by the image of $f-g$.],[The map $(a,b)|->a+b$ has the required kernel.],[The image of $f-g$ is the line spanned by $(1,-1)$. The quotient is one-dimensional and is identified with $K$ by $(a,b)|->a+b$, whose kernel is that line and which is surjective. This map coequalizes $f,g$ and has the quotient universal property.],[The formula also works in characteristic two.]),
 p(4,"C","U6 Representability",[Show the functor $G |-> G times G$ from groups to sets is represented by a free group on two generators.],[A homomorphism from a free group is determined by generator images.],[Any ordered pair of group elements is permitted.],[Let $F_2$ be free on $a,b$. Sending a homomorphism to $(f(a),f(b))$ is bijective by the free-group property. Postcomposition applies a group homomorphism to both entries, so this bijection is natural. Hence the underlying-pair functor is represented by $F_2$.],[Use the free-group universal property, not the direct product of two copies of $ZZ$.]),
 p(5,"A","U2–U5 Equivalence",[Let $F$ be full, faithful, and essentially surjective. Prove it sends a terminal object to a terminal object.],[Represent an arbitrary target-category object up to isomorphism by $F A$.],[Use the hom-set bijection supplied by full faithfulness.],[For any target object $D$, choose an isomorphism $F A -> D$. Maps $D -> F T$ correspond by precomposition to maps $F A -> F T$, and full faithfulness identifies the latter with maps $A -> T$. Since $T$ is terminal, this set is a singleton. Thus every $D$ has exactly one map to $F T$, proving terminality.],[Explain why essential surjectivity is needed to cover every $D$.]),
 p(6,"A","U7–U8 Logic",[In the power-set poset of $S$, identify a right adjoint to intersection with a fixed $A$ and derive its counit inequality.],[Solve $X inter A subset.eq B$ for the largest permissible $X$.],[The largest choice is $(S without A) union B$.],[The right adjoint is $G(B)=(S without A) union B$, since $X inter A subset.eq B$ iff $X subset.eq G(B)$. Its counit is $G(B) inter A subset.eq B$, which holds because an element in $A$ cannot be in its complement and must therefore belong to $B$.],[State the adjunction equivalence and derive the correctly oriented inequality.]),
)
